  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>An important note upfront</h2>
      <p>The "Lac de l'Écureuil" no longer exists as a body of water: the tourist office pages speak of an "Écureuil site (dried-up expanse)" and a "former Lac de l'Écureuil, now dry". What you find by following the trail is the Ravin du Mal Infernet (or Malinfernet), which runs alongside a stream and crosses a small canyon of red rock, in the heart of a biological reserve. The name "lac" is still used out of habit, but don't expect to swim there.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The route is about 7.2 km round trip with 74 m of elevation gain, easy level, 3h30 announced on the circuits.esterel-cotedazur.com page, marked "animals accepted"; the Destination Côte d'Azur page specifies "on a leash only", and the Saint-Raphaël tourist office writes on its massif page that dogs are allowed there, kept on a leash. The trail is free and open year-round, daily, weather permitting. It crosses a biological reserve; a marked mountain-bike loop also starts from Col de Belle Barbe (12 km, 250 m of elevation gain on the circuits.esterel-cotedazur.com page), so bikes may share the Malinfernet track.</p>
      <p>The terrain mixes rock, earth and fine gravel. The track first follows the Grenouillet stream; after a good half hour you cross it at a ford, then the landscape becomes more enclosed, with a small canyon of red rock, the "Malinfernet canyon". A second ford follows, and the last few metres are the only climb of the route before the Écureuil site, a dried-up expanse. Both fords can be submerged after heavy rain: the tourist office advises being able to take your shoes off or protecting them with plastic bags, which also applies to your dog's paws if the water is high. To get there from Agay, go under the railway bridge, turn right onto the small "Massif de l'Estérel" road, go past the Gratadis forest lodge, then turn left towards "Col de Belle Barbe" and park in the car park after the climb. You return by the same route.</p>
      <p>As for the massif's rules, the Saint-Raphaël tourist office reminds visitors that lighting any fire in the forest is forbidden all year round and that vehicle traffic is banned from 9pm to 6am.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: from June to September, check the massif access map every day on risque-prevention-incendie.fr/var, published before 7pm for the next day; in strong mistral winds the massif can also be closed to all traffic by prefectoral decision.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Heading back down towards Fréjus, the <a href="/en/fiche-frejus-argens">Plage de Fréjus-Argens</a>, a <a href="/en/fiche-toiletteur-la-calinerie-frejus">groomer</a> and a <a href="/en/fiche-veterinaire-des-arenes-frejus">vet</a> on site make it easy to combine outings, and <a href="/en/fiche-lac-saintcassien">Lake Saint-Cassien</a> rounds out the waterside walking options in the area.</p>
